1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,537
Okay. Who wants to share? And Tuna, "my horoscope" is not a share.

2
00:00:05,887 --> 00:00:08,221
It was a really rough horoscope, though.

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:13,318
I'll go. Last time I didn't go, the judge added three more weeks.

4
00:00:14,668 --> 00:00:16,617
That's not how it works, Mikey.

5
00:00:16,967 --> 00:00:20,431
You say that. But I've seen the paperwork. It's very vague.

6
00:00:20,781 --> 00:00:23,735
Okay. So. This actually happened.

7
00:00:25,085 --> 00:00:27,318
It did. I was there. I'm the hero.

8
00:00:27,668 --> 00:00:30,056
There's no hero, Tuna. There's a goose.

9
00:00:30,406 --> 00:00:35,240
I've been doing this for twenty-two years. I've never once been excited about a goose.

10
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:38,092
Then buckle up, Pam.

11
00:00:38,442 --> 00:00:43,839
Tuna, these chairs don't have seatbelts. Last session you asked me that four times.

12
00:00:44,189 --> 00:00:46,423
And I'm still not comfortable with the answer.

13
00:00:50,973 --> 00:00:58,380
Tuesday. Eleven p.m. My mom's basement. Which I call "my apartment," and which she calls "the basement."

14
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:05,725
Earlier that week, Gus had fronted us some of his stuff. I'm not going to say what. My lawyer says I'm not going to say what.

15
00:01:06,075 --> 00:01:10,929
And then we lost it. Not sold it. Not used it. Lost it. To a goose.

16
00:01:11,279 --> 00:01:15,396
That goose knew exactly what it was doing, Mikey. It made eye contact.

17
00:01:15,746 --> 00:01:20,764
You put it in your bucket hat. At the duck pond. Then you took the hat off to wave at the ducks.

18
00:01:21,114 --> 00:01:22,405
They looked lonely.

19
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:33,286
Gentlemen. Peace be with you. And also, six hundred dollars be with me. By sunrise.

20
00:01:33,636 --> 00:01:36,789
Sunrise? Gus, we said "end of the month."

21
00:01:37,139 --> 00:01:42,424
Mikey, I used to be a youth pastor. You know what I learned? Every day is the end of somebody's month.

22
00:01:43,774 --> 00:01:46,096
That's beautiful. Is it in the Bible?

23
00:01:46,446 --> 00:01:49,797
It's on a mug I sell. Twelve bucks. I'll add it to your tab.

24
00:01:50,147 --> 00:01:55,488
Gus, a goose took it. That's an act of God. You were a pastor. You know the rules.

25
00:01:56,838 --> 00:01:59,935
God doesn't front, Tuna. I front. Different department.

26
00:02:00,285 --> 00:02:03,434
Gus, what happens if we don't have it by sunrise?

27
00:02:03,784 --> 00:02:09,971
Nothing dramatic, son. I just stop being your friend. And I'm a very bad person to not be friends with.

28
00:02:13,549 --> 00:02:19,448
For the record, he was never our friend. He just knew our birthdays. Which is somehow worse.

29
00:02:19,798 --> 00:02:22,349
Mikey. Relax. I have a plan.

30
00:02:22,699 --> 00:02:26,942
Tuna, you always have a plan. Your plans are why we have a court date.

31
00:02:28,292 --> 00:02:31,722
My plans are visionary, Mikey. The world just isn't ready.

32
00:02:32,072 --> 00:02:35,762
Here's a partial list of things the world wasn't ready for.

33
00:02:36,312 --> 00:02:39,996
Uber, but for borrowing your neighbor's lawnmower without asking.

34
00:02:40,346 --> 00:02:44,504
Gluten-free gravel. For people who are already eating gravel.

35
00:02:44,854 --> 00:02:49,410
A haunted house. In my actual house. While my grandma was home. She's still not talking to me.

36
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,771
Okay. What's the plan?

37
00:02:54,121 --> 00:02:58,772
Step one. We find six hundred dollars. Step two. We give it to Gus.

38
00:02:59,122 --> 00:03:02,954
Tuna. That's not a plan. That's the problem, read out loud.

39
00:03:03,304 --> 00:03:06,507
Every plan is a problem read out loud with confidence.

40
00:03:07,857 --> 00:03:11,167
And that was the scariest part. He wasn't wrong.

41
00:03:11,717 --> 00:03:19,843
Step one of step one. We went upstairs. My mom was on a double shift at the hospital. Which meant the house was, legally, unsupervised.

42
00:03:20,193 --> 00:03:22,908
The microwave! People always need a microwave!

43
00:03:24,258 --> 00:03:29,774
We need the microwave, Tuna. It's the only thing in this house that still works on the first try.

44
00:03:30,124 --> 00:03:33,088
Then it'll sell fast. That's just supply and demand.

45
00:03:33,438 --> 00:03:36,574
What about your stuff? You have a whole room at your grandma's.

46
00:03:37,924 --> 00:03:42,668
Everything I own is in a haunted house, Mikey. Nobody's buying from a haunted house.

47
00:03:43,018 --> 00:03:45,859
It's not haunted. You just did a haunted house in it.

48
00:03:46,209 --> 00:03:48,538
And I did it so well, it stuck.

49
00:03:49,888 --> 00:03:56,706
We took the microwave. And her good lamp. And a little box from her dresser that I told myself I'd buy back by Friday.

50
00:03:57,056 --> 00:04:02,622
I'd been telling myself things like that since I was fifteen. I'm very persuasive. To me.

51
00:04:03,172 --> 00:04:10,947
Welcome to Gary's Pre-Owned Dreams. Open twenty-four hours. Because desperation doesn't sleep. And neither do I. Medically.

52
00:04:12,297 --> 00:04:17,170
One microwave. Barely used. Only for popcorn and one time a sock.

53
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:18,710
Eight dollars.

54
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:21,746
Eight? It has a popcorn button!

55
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:27,590
Everything has a popcorn button now, Mikey. It's not special. Nothing is special.

56
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:32,116
And this. It's a ring. It was my grandma's.

57
00:04:32,466 --> 00:04:36,986
Ninety dollars. And I'm going to need your mom's number. For the paperwork.

58
00:04:38,336 --> 00:04:40,326
Why do you need my mom's number?

59
00:04:40,676 --> 00:04:44,737
Because I've done this for a long time, kid. And moms always come in on Thursday.

60
00:04:45,887 --> 00:04:50,166
And the lamp? It's a very good lamp. It's my favorite lamp I've ever stolen.

61
00:04:51,516 --> 00:04:56,207
I'll give you nothing for the lamp. But I'll take it off your hands. As a favor to lamps.

62
00:04:56,557 --> 00:05:02,909
Total: ninety-eight dollars. Five hundred and two to go. And one less lamp in the world, which Gary called "a mercy."

63
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:14,087
One-thirty a.m. Walking down Main Street. My body was doing the thing it does when Gus's stuff runs out. I felt like a phone at two percent that somebody keeps opening apps on.

64
00:05:14,437 --> 00:05:16,400
You okay, man? You're shaking.

65
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:17,878
I'm cold.

66
00:05:18,228 --> 00:05:19,908
It's seventy degrees.

67
00:05:21,258 --> 00:05:23,993
I'm cold on the inside, Tuna. Let's keep moving.

68
00:05:24,343 --> 00:05:30,231
That was a lie. I told a lot of them that night. That one I told to the only person who'd have believed me anyway.

69
00:05:30,781 --> 00:05:36,750
Two a.m. The gas station on Route 9. Tuna's next idea was a charity car wash.

70
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:38,703
Tuna, which charity?

71
00:05:39,053 --> 00:05:41,873
Us. We're the charity. Look at us, Mikey.

72
00:05:42,223 --> 00:05:45,886
That's the first fully honest business you've ever started.

73
00:05:47,236 --> 00:05:51,082
Car wash! Car wash! We have one sponge and a lot of heart!

74
00:05:51,432 --> 00:05:53,640
Two a.m.! No questions asked!

75
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:57,725
Stop saying "no questions asked." That's why nobody's stopping.

76
00:05:59,075 --> 00:06:05,593
In forty minutes, one car pulled in. It was a hearse. The driver wanted directions. We charged him four dollars.

77
00:06:05,943 --> 00:06:09,250
We just charged a hearse for directions to a cemetery.

78
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:12,548
He got there, didn't he? That's a satisfied customer.

79
00:06:13,898 --> 00:06:16,081
The passenger didn't seem satisfied.

80
00:06:16,431 --> 00:06:20,690
The passenger didn't seem anything, Mikey. That's kind of the passenger's whole deal.

81
00:06:25,207 --> 00:06:30,020
Evening, boys. Somebody called in two men "aggressively sponging" at the pump.

82
00:06:31,370 --> 00:06:34,524
Officer Gutierrez! Has your car been washed tonight?

83
00:06:34,874 --> 00:06:39,725
My car has never been washed. It's held together by the dirt. Like a bird's nest.

84
00:06:40,075 --> 00:06:44,335
And that's when I had my idea. Which I want to be clear was worse than Tuna's.

85
00:06:44,685 --> 00:06:48,490
Officer, have you considered an extended warranty on your vehicle?

86
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:52,500
Oh my God. It's you. You're the ones who keep calling me.

87
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:56,032
That's not us. But we respect their hustle.

88
00:06:57,382 --> 00:07:02,190
For only fifty dollars, if anything breaks on your cruiser, we will feel really bad about it.

89
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:03,854
What does that cover?

90
00:07:04,204 --> 00:07:09,158
Emotionally? Everything. Financially? Nothing. It's very comprehensive.

91
00:07:10,508 --> 00:07:13,272
Does it cover the time I backed into the Mayor?

92
00:07:13,622 --> 00:07:16,498
Especially that. We think about it all the time.

93
00:07:16,848 --> 00:07:20,379
You know, nobody ever feels bad when my car breaks. I'll take it.

94
00:07:21,729 --> 00:07:25,018
Hang on. I'm legally required to run your names first.

95
00:07:25,368 --> 00:07:28,185
Bad news. The warranty just expired.

96
00:07:28,535 --> 00:07:31,929
Have a great night, Officer! Drive safe! It's not covered!

97
00:07:38,041 --> 00:07:42,578
They left their sponge. That's the nicest thing anyone's left me in years.

98
00:07:43,128 --> 00:07:50,352
Three a.m. Total: one hundred and two dollars. Tuna said old people always have cash. I want to say I stopped him.

99
00:07:50,702 --> 00:07:53,334
I did not stop him. I held the flashlight.

100
00:07:53,684 --> 00:07:58,342
Mr. Earl, sir. I'm a lawyer. Your long-lost cousin died and left you a fortune.

101
00:07:58,692 --> 00:07:59,747
Which cousin?

102
00:08:00,097 --> 00:08:02,867
Your cousin... Cousin... Mikey, what's a cousin name?

103
00:08:04,217 --> 00:08:06,626
Don't look at me. You said you'd been practicing.

104
00:08:06,976 --> 00:08:08,815
The... long-lost one.

105
00:08:09,165 --> 00:08:14,542
Oh, Benny. Benny got lost in a corn maze in fifty-eight. Nobody ever went in after him.

106
00:08:15,892 --> 00:08:21,289
Right. Benny. To release the fortune, there's a small processing fee. One hundred dollars.

107
00:08:21,639 --> 00:08:26,682
Benny never had a fortune. Benny had a very nice harmonica and a lot of opinions about Canada.

108
00:08:27,232 --> 00:08:32,049
We heard that harmonica for eleven years. Every night. Coming from the corn.

109
00:08:32,399 --> 00:08:38,457
It was always the same song. Mostly the wrong notes. We put out soup. The soup was always gone.

110
00:08:40,207 --> 00:08:42,231
So is Benny alive or not?

111
00:08:42,581 --> 00:08:50,077
Here's the thing, young lawyer. Benny owed me forty dollars. If you represent his estate, you owe me forty dollars.

112
00:08:51,427 --> 00:08:55,211
Then I decline the case. I'm an ex-lawyer now. As of right now.

113
00:08:55,561 --> 00:09:00,726
Too late. You already said "estate." That's binding. My lawn, my rules.

114
00:09:01,076 --> 00:09:03,128
That's not how inheritance works.

115
00:09:04,478 --> 00:09:11,653
It's how it works on my lawn. I've got a shotgun and a very good memory for faces. Hello, Mikey. Say hi to Linda.

116
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:20,649
Total: sixty-two dollars. We had gone to scam an old man and had been successfully scammed by an old man. And he knew my mom.

117
00:09:21,199 --> 00:09:29,457
Five-forty a.m. Sunrise was at six-twelve. Gus was eating pancakes like a man who has never once owed anybody anything.

118
00:09:29,807 --> 00:09:36,684
Gus. We have sixty-two dollars. And a sponge. Actually, no. We left the sponge.

119
00:09:37,034 --> 00:09:39,811
Sixty-two. Out of six hundred and twelve.

120
00:09:40,161 --> 00:09:42,898
Twelve? Where'd the twelve come from?

121
00:09:44,248 --> 00:09:47,890
The mug, son. I told you. Every day is the end of somebody's month.

122
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:53,356
But we have a business now. A car wash. And a warranty company. And a law firm. Briefly.

123
00:09:53,706 --> 00:09:58,834
Three businesses in one night. Tuna, that's not an empire. That's a crime spree with a logo.

124
00:10:00,184 --> 00:10:05,323
Gus, please. One more week. I'll get a job. A real one. With a vest.

125
00:10:05,673 --> 00:10:12,166
I'll take the sixty-two. You've got till Sunday. And Mikey? Your mom's working doubles. Don't make me see her at the hospital.

126
00:10:13,516 --> 00:10:15,539
As a patient, or a visitor?

127
00:10:16,889 --> 00:10:19,935
Either. I'm very flexible. God bless, boys.

128
00:10:22,071 --> 00:10:23,827
Mikey. Was that a threat?

129
00:10:24,177 --> 00:10:27,744
He blessed us on the way out, Tuna. That's how you know.

130
00:10:29,094 --> 00:10:35,338
That was the moment the night stopped being funny. Which, if you've been keeping track, was about six hours late.

131
00:10:35,888 --> 00:10:42,855
Seven a.m. I got home. Tuna went to his grandma's. She still isn't talking to him, but she lets him sit there.

132
00:10:43,205 --> 00:10:47,881
I figured I had an hour before my mom got off her shift. I was wrong about that too.

133
00:10:51,803 --> 00:10:57,442
There's a rectangle on the counter where the microwave used to be. It's the cleanest part of my kitchen.

134
00:10:57,792 --> 00:11:01,756
Mom. You're home. I thought you were on till eight.

135
00:11:02,106 --> 00:11:08,695
A man named Gary called me at four in the morning. He asked if I'd like to buy back my mother's ring. "At a very reasonable markup."

136
00:11:10,045 --> 00:11:12,181
I was going to get it back by Friday.

137
00:11:12,531 --> 00:11:15,800
I know, baby. You're always going to get it back by Friday.

138
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:21,109
Are you gonna yell? You can yell. I'd honestly prefer the yelling.

139
00:11:22,459 --> 00:11:27,881
I worked sixteen hours, Mikey. I don't have a yell left. I spent it on a man in bed nine.

140
00:11:28,231 --> 00:11:33,292
I paid Gary. With my overtime. The ring's in my pocket. The microwave, he can keep.

141
00:11:33,642 --> 00:11:35,698
I'm sorry. I'm really sorry.

142
00:11:37,048 --> 00:11:42,171
Earl called too. At five. He said you owe him forty dollars for a cousin named Benny.

143
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:43,628
Did you pay him?

144
00:11:43,978 --> 00:11:48,721
No. Earl doesn't have a cousin named Benny. Earl has a goat named Benny.

145
00:11:50,071 --> 00:11:53,114
Look at me. Are you okay? Not "fine." Okay.

146
00:11:54,964 --> 00:11:55,708
No.

147
00:11:56,058 --> 00:12:00,346
Okay. That's the first true thing you've said to me in a month. We can start with that.

148
00:12:01,696 --> 00:12:08,452
She made me toast. In the toaster. Because we didn't have a microwave. That was the whole lesson, I think. I'm still working on it.

149
00:12:09,002 --> 00:12:11,614
So. That's it. That actually happened.

150
00:12:11,964 --> 00:12:17,669
And the car wash is still technically open. So, if anyone here has a car that needs washing...

151
00:12:19,019 --> 00:12:24,855
Tuna, this is court-ordered therapy. You cannot do sales in court-ordered therapy.

152
00:12:25,205 --> 00:12:27,075
Then why is there a donut table?

153
00:12:27,425 --> 00:12:33,145
Mikey. Let me make sure I followed. You lost something that wasn't yours, to a goose.

154
00:12:34,495 --> 00:12:42,520
Then you sold things that weren't yours, to pay a man who isn't your friend, and you ended the night owing an old man for a cousin who lives in a corn maze.

155
00:12:42,870 --> 00:12:45,685
When you say it like that, it sounds bad.

156
00:12:46,035 --> 00:12:48,825
It's the only way to say it, Mikey. I checked.

157
00:12:50,175 --> 00:12:58,003
But the only thing you actually earned that night was the word "no" in your mom's kitchen. That one's yours. Don't pawn it.

158
00:12:59,353 --> 00:13:02,096
Gary would only give me eight bucks for it anyway.

159
00:13:02,446 --> 00:13:03,590
And Gus?

160
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:12,885
My mom drove me to his place. She waited in the car with the engine running. I paid him the rest out of my first paycheck. I bag groceries now. With a vest.

161
00:13:13,235 --> 00:13:16,010
And I got a job at the duck pond. Security.

162
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:18,017
Against the goose.

163
00:13:19,367 --> 00:13:21,106
It's personal now, Pam.

164
00:13:21,456 --> 00:13:25,026
Okay. Same time next week. Tuna, put the new sponge away.

165
00:13:25,376 --> 00:13:30,178
Pam, it's not a car wash. It's a new idea. A car wash for feelings.

166
00:13:31,528 --> 00:13:34,774
That's just this, Tuna. That's what this is.

167
00:13:35,124 --> 00:13:38,581
And you charge the state for it? Pam. You're a genius.

